{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013a2bG2rhkm4jR6gjWfXjRV","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→{\n     2→  \"metadata\": {\n     3→    \"title\": \"The Cask of Amontillado - Poe\",\n     4→    \"author\": \"Edgar Allan Poe\",\n     5→    \"source_file\": \"Test_Texts/The Cask of Amontillado - Poe.txt\",\n     6→    \"source_format\": \"txt\",\n     7→    \"total_word_count\": 2358,\n     8→    \"total_character_count\": 13023,\n     9→    \"estimated_total_duration_minutes\": 15.693333333333333,\n    10→    \"words_per_minute\": 150\n    11→  },\n    12→  \"structure\": [\n    13→    {\n    14→      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n    15→      \"title\": null,\n    16→      \"index\": 1,\n    17→      \"start_position\": 24,\n    18→      \"end_position\": 13023,\n    19→      \"word_count\": 2354,\n    20→      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 15.693333333333333,\n    21→      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n    22→      \"children\": [],\n    23→      \"characters_present\": [\n    24→        \"Montresor\",\n    25→        \"Fortunato\"\n    26→      ],\n    27→      \"summary\": \"The chapter opens with the narrator, Montresor, vowing revenge against Fortunato for an unspecified insult, resolving to punish him with impunity during the chaotic carnival season. At dusk, Montresor lures the drunken, costumed Fortunato—wearing a jingling motley outfit and conical cap—through the damp, bone-strewn catacombs beneath his palazzo by feigning concern over a pipe of supposed Amontillado and invoking the rival wine expert Luchresi. As they descend deeper into the vaults, Montresor offers wine to mask the rising nitre and feigns concern for Fortunato’s cough, all while subtly guiding him toward a hidden niche. Once there, Montresor chains Fortunato to the wall with a padlock and begins sealing him alive behind a stone wall, layer by layer, as Fortunato’s initial laughter turns to desperate pleas and finally silence. Montresor completes the wall, reassembles the original bone pile, and leaves the body undisturbed for fifty years.\"\n    28→    }\n    29→  ],\n    30→  \"characters\": [\n    31→    {\n    32→      \"id\": \"char_amontillado_1b7d65f7\",\n    33→      \"canonical_name\": \"Amontillado\",\n    34→      \"aliases\": [],\n    35→      \"role\": null,\n    36→      \"appearance\": null,\n    37→      \"personality\": null,\n    38→      \"voice_guidance\": null,\n    39→      \"descriptions\": [\n    40→        {\n    41→          \"text\": \"The character is the first-person narrator of the story and serves as the storyteller. They express doubt about the authenticity of a pipe of Amontillado and state they paid the full price without consulting Fortunato. The narrator repeatedly refers to 'Amontillado' as a wine, and insists on verifying its authenticity by seeking a critical opinion from Luchresi. The narrator guides Fortunato through a crypt to reach the Amontillado, and at the end, they humorously confirm the name of the wine as a joke, suggesting a tone of irony and self-awareness. The narrator's role is central to the narrative structure, as they recount events and maintain a consistent voice throughout the story.\",\n    42→          \"source_position\": 2046,\n    43→          \"chapter_index\": null,\n    44→          \"confidence\": \"llm_refined\"\n    45→        }\n    46→      ],\n    47→      \"relationships\": {},\n    48→      \"evidence\": [\n    49→        {\n    50→          \"statement\": \"The narrator expresses doubt about the authenticity of a pipe of Amontillado\",\n    51→          \"quote\": \"I have my doubts\",\n    52→          \"position\": 2100,\n    53→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    54→        },\n    55→        {\n    56→          \"statement\": \"The narrator paid the full Amontillado price without consulting Fortunato\",\n    57→          \"quote\": \"I was silly enough to pay the full Amontillado price without consulting you in the matter\",\n    58→          \"position\": 2365,\n    59→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    60→        },\n    61→        {\n    62→          \"statement\": \"The narrator intends to verify the wine's authenticity by consulting Luchresi\",\n    63→          \"quote\": \"If any one has a critical turn it is he. He will tell me --\",\n    64→          \"position\": 2402,\n    65→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    66→        },\n    67→        {\n    68→          \"statement\": \"The narrator and Fortunato proceed to search for the Amontillado in a crypt\",\n    69→          \"quote\": \"We continued our route in search of the Amontillado\",\n    70→          \"position\": 7442,\n    71→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    72→        },\n    73→        {\n    74→          \"statement\": \"The narrator confirms the name of the wine as a joke at the end\",\n    75→          \"quote\": \"The Amontillado! I said. He! he! he! --he! he! he! --yes, the Amontillado\",\n    76→          \"position\": 12110,\n    77→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    78→        }\n    79→      ],\n    80→      \"voice_notes\": null,\n    81→      \"first_appearance_chapter\": 1,\n    82→      \"mention_count\": 16,\n    83→      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    84→      \"is_narrator\": false,\n    85→      \"narrative_role\": null\n    86→    },\n    87→    {\n    88→      \"id\": \"char_fortunato_b564181f\",\n    89→      \"canonical_name\": \"Fortunato\",\n    90→      \"aliases\": [],\n    91→      \"role\": null,\n    92→      \"appearance\": null,\n    93→      \"personality\": null,\n    94→      \"voice_guidance\": null,\n    95→      \"descriptions\": [\n    96→        {\n    97→          \"text\": \"Fortunato is a wine connoisseur who claims sincerity in his expertise, particularly regarding old wines, and is portrayed as a figure of social pretension who is manipulated by Montresor's deception. He is initially presented as a comical, drunken figure in a costumed outfit, and his laughter during the final moments of the narrative reveals a sense of mockery and delusion, indicating that he is unaware of the true nature of the situation. His actions and responses are entirely reactive, showing no resistance or awareness of the danger until it is too late.\",\n    98→          \"source_position\": 88,\n    99→          \"chapter_index\": null,\n   100→          \"confidence\": \"llm_refined\"\n   101→        }\n   102→      ],\n   103→      \"relationships\": {},\n   104→      \"evidence\": [\n   105→        {\n   106→          \"statement\": \"Fortunato is a wine connoisseur who claims sincerity in his expertise\",\n   107→          \"quote\": \"In painting and gemmary, Fortunato, like his countrymen, was a quack, but in the matter of old wines he was sincere. In this respect I did not differ from him materially; --I was skillful in the Italian vintages myself, and...\",\n   108→          \"position\": 1271,\n   109→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n   110→        },\n   111→        {\n   112→          \"statement\": \"Fortunato is portrayed as a drunken, costumed figure\",\n   113→          \"quote\": \"At dusk, Montresor lures the drunken, costumed Fortunato—wearing a jingling motley outfit and conical cap—through the damp, bone-strewn catacombs beneath his palazzo by feigning concern over a pipe of supposed Amontillado and invoking the rival wine expert Luchresi.\",\n   114→          \"position\": 1271,\n   115→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n   116→        },\n   117→        {\n   118→          \"statement\": \"Fortunato laughs mockingly during the final moments of the narrative\",\n   119→          \"quote\": \"Ha! ha! ha! --he! he! he! --a very good joke, indeed --an excellent jest. We will have many a rich laugh about it at the palazzo --he! he! he! --over our wine --he! he! he!...\",\n   120→          \"position\": 12266,\n   121→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n   122→        },\n   123→        {\n   124→          \"statement\": \"Fort