189→      \"description\": \"This book contains 1 chapters. Chapters are numbered sequentially.\"\n  1190→    },\n  1191→    \"plot_summary\": {\n  1192→      \"plot_summary\": \"Amontillado, the story's narrator, begins by recounting his deep-seated grudge against Fortunato, a man who has insulted him in a way that wounds his pride. Though the exact nature of the insult is never revealed, Amontillado is determined to exact revenge with complete impunity, a vow he will fulfill during the chaotic carnival season when the world is distracted and the boundaries between reality and illusion blur. He crafts a scheme to lure Fortunato, a man known for his taste in wine and his arrogance, into the catacombs beneath his palazzo by pretending to possess a rare and exquisite wine called Amontillado, which he claims to have discovered and which he believes only Fortunato can authenticate.\\n\\nAs they descend through the damp, bone-strewn catacombs, Amontillado plays on Fortunato’s vanity and his love of wine, feigning concern over a pipe of Amontillado and invoking the reputation of the rival wine expert Luchresi to add credibility to his claim. He offers wine to mask the rising stench of nitre and subtly guides Fortunato deeper into the vaults, toward a hidden niche. The atmosphere grows increasingly oppressive, and as Fortunato grows more aware of the danger, his initial laughter turns into desperate pleas. Amontillado, cold and methodical, chains Fortunato to the wall and begins sealing him alive behind a stone wall, layer by layer, with meticulous precision, ensuring that Fortunato is trapped in silence and darkness.\\n\\nThe climax occurs when Amontillado completes the wall, reassembles the original bone pile to restore the catacombs’ appearance, and leaves Fortunato’s body undisturbed for fifty years. The story ends with Amontillado reflecting on the success of his revenge, emphasizing that he has achieved his goal with perfect impunity. The key relationship between Amontillado and Fortunato is defined by a personal betrayal and a transformation from adversaries to a chillingly intimate act of vengeance. The turning point is the moment when Fortunato’s laughter fades into silence, marking the irreversible end of his life and the fulfillment of Amontillado’s vow.\\n\",\n  1193→      \"themes\": [\n  1194→        \"revenge\",\n  1195→        \"isolation\",\n  1196→        \"moral ambiguity\"\n  1197→      ],\n  1198→      \"narrative_style\": \"first-person retrospective\"\n  1199→    },\n  1200→    \"model_usage\": {\n  1201→      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n  1202→        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n  1203→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1204→      },\n  1205→      \"Character Extraction\": {\n  1206→        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n  1207→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1208→      },\n  1209→      \"Chapter Summaries\": {\n  1210→        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n  1211→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1212→      },\n  1213→      \"Character Profiles\": {\n  1214→        \"model\": \"qwen3:4b-instruct\",\n  1215→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1216→      },\n  1217→      \"Pronunciation Guide\": {\n  1218→        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n  1219→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1220→      }\n  1221→    },\n  1222→    \"timing\": {\n  1223→      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n  1224→        \"duration_seconds\": 67.72,\n  1225→        \"duration_formatted\": \"1m 8s\"\n  1226→      },\n  1227→      \"Character Extraction\": {\n  1228→        \"duration_seconds\": 100.04,\n  1229→        \"duration_formatted\": \"1m 40s\"\n  1230→      },\n  1231→      \"Chapter Summaries\": {\n  1232→        \"duration_seconds\": 29.65,\n  1233→        \"duration_formatted\": \"29.6s\"\n  1234→      },\n  1235→      \"Character Profiles\": {\n  1236→        \"duration_seconds\": 107.59,\n  1237→        \"duration_formatted\": \"1m 48s\"\n  1238→      },\n  1239→      \"P